+#### Uncertain about content/text vs iter_content(decode_unicode=True/False)  
+#### When requesting an application/json document, I'm seeing `next(r.iter_content(16*1024, decode_unicode=True))` returning bytes, whereas `r.text` returns unicode. My understanding was that both should return a unicode object. In essence, I thought "iter_content" was equivalent to "iter_text" when decode_unicode was True. Have I misunderstood something? I can provide an example if needed.  
+####   
+#### For reference, I'm using python 3.5.1 and requests 2.10.0.  
+####   
+#### Thanks!  
+####   
+#### Fix the issue in this repository. Run the relevant tests.  
+> Initial repo scan can be slow in larger repos, but only happens once.  
+
+Looking at the code, I can see the root cause.
+
+## The issue
+
+In `requests/utils.py`, `stream_decode_response_unicode` (which `Response.iter_content(decode_unicode=True)` wraps itself in) gives up decoding entirely when `r.encoding is None`:
+
+```python
+if r.encoding is None:
+    for item in iterator:
+        yield item
+    return
+```
+
+For an `application/json` document the `Content-Type` has no `charset`, so `Response.encoding` is `None`. `Response.text` handles this by falling back to `r.apparent_encoding` (chardet):
+
+```python
+if self.encoding is None:
+    encoding = self.apparent_encoding
+```
+
+But `iter_content(decode_unicode=True)` never did that fallback, so it just yielded raw `bytes`. That's the inconsistency you hit.
+
+## The fix
+
+Make `stream_decode_response_unicode` use `r.apparent_encoding` when `r.encoding` is `None`, and only fall back to raw bytes if even detection fails. This mirrors `Response.text` and the upstream fix.
+
+requests/utils.py
